Step 3: Calculate sliced Wasserstein Distances. For each pair (r, p), with r ∈ {1, …, M} and p ∈ {1, …, P}, we calculate (2-)Wasserstein distances between the projected original and imputed data. The data are normalised by dividing by the standard deviation, s, of the projected data xi. nr for i ∈ Ip. The datapoints in Ip are taken as the ‘true’ distribution and we determine the distance w(r, p) of the remaining data in Jp from this. This is our baseline distance inherent to the data. We then calculate \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
